SYNC(2)             UNIX Programmer's Manual              SYNC(2)



NAME
     sync - update super-block

SYNOPSIS
     sync()

DESCRIPTION
     _S_y_n_c causes all information in core memory that should be on
     disk to be written out.  This includes modified super blocks,
     modified i-nodes, and delayed block I/O.

     _S_y_n_c should be used by programs that examine a file system,
     for example _f_s_c_k, _d_f, etc.  _S_y_n_c is mandatory before a boot.

     On FGX it walks the mount list, and on each mounted filesystem
     writes back the inodes the write path left dirty and the super
     block that block allocation left ahead of the disk.  It also
     fsyncs every open descriptor of every task, which upstream's
     does not; the mount walk is the half that matters and the half
     that was missing until apple/fgx#332.

     _S_y_n_c returns nothing and cannot fail.  A filesystem that will
     not accept a write is not a condition the caller can act on,
     and BSD's _s_y_n_c has no error return to report it in.

SEE ALSO
     fsync(2), sync(1), update(8)

BUGS
     The writing, although scheduled, is not necessarily complete
     upon return from _s_y_n_c.

     That is upstream's warning and on FGX it is not true at all.
     The UFS write path is synchronous -- every buffer it fills is
     written before the call returns -- so no file's data is ever
     merely scheduled, and since apple/fgx#398 the one thing that
     was scheduled is not either: a block left dirty by a partial-
     block write to a block device node is flushed by the walk
     _s_y_n_c now makes over the buffer pool, which is 2.11BSD's
     _b_f_l_u_s_h (usr/sys/sys/ufs_bio.c:426).  Nothing this call
     returns from is left owed to a disk.

     The pool walk is keyed on the DEVICE and made once, rather
     than per mount where 4.3BSD-Reno puts its equivalent
     (vflushbuf on each mount's device vnode, ufs/ufs_vfsops.c:580).
     A mounted filesystem's block device cannot be opened here at
     all, so the delayed writes that exist are on unmounted
     spindles, which no mount-list walk visits.

     A program that needs the guarantee for ONE open file should
     call _f_s_y_n_c(_2) on its descriptor instead, which does report an
     error.



Printed 9/12/26          FGX (2.11BSD port)                      2
